From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] Revert "perf annotate: Fix build with NO_SLANG=1"

This reverts stable commit e97bd4417010c648acf9b1e509cfb77fc506e09e,
which first shipped in 6.18.52 and which breaks every NO_SLANG=1 build
of perf -- including Buildroot's, which is the only kind this image
ever does.

The reverted commit is a half-backport. In mainline it is correct: it
repairs the `#else` (no-slang) side of tools/perf/util/hist.h after
ad83f3b7155db28e82de24dbaa1af2b8f5d972a3 ("perf c2c annotate: Start
from the contention line", Tianyou Li) gave __hist_entry__tui_annotate()
and hist_entry__tui_annotate() a fifth/fourth `u64 al_addr` parameter on
the HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT side, put `#define NO_ADDR 0` INSIDE that #ifdef,
and updated every caller to pass NO_ADDR or a real address.

Mainline's repair is therefore two things: it MOVES `#define NO_ADDR 0`
from inside `#ifdef HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT` to above it, and it adds
`al_addr` to the two `#else` stubs. 6.18.y took the repair and never
took what it repairs, so the move had nothing to move -- it degenerated
into a bare addition of a macro nothing uses -- and the two inline stubs
became the only things in the whole tree that believe in `al_addr`:

  $ grep -rn 'NO_ADDR' tools/            # linux-6.18.52
  tools/perf/util/hist.h:712:#define NO_ADDR 0

  $ grep -rn 'hist_entry__tui_annotate' tools/perf/
  builtin-annotate.c:523:      key = hist_entry__tui_annotate(he, evsel, NULL);
  ui/browsers/annotate.c:611:  __hist_entry__tui_annotate(browser->he, &target_ms, evsel, hbt);
  ui/browsers/annotate.c:1111: int hist_entry__tui_annotate(struct hist_entry *he, struct evsel *evsel,
  ui/browsers/annotate.c:1119:     return __hist_entry__tui_annotate(he, &he->ms, evsel, hbt);
  ui/browsers/annotate.c:1122: int __hist_entry__tui_annotate(struct hist_entry *he, struct map_symbol *ms,
  ui/browsers/hists.c:2488:    err = __hist_entry__tui_annotate(he, &act->ms, evsel, browser->hbt);
  util/hist.h:718,722                     <- HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT prototypes, 4 and 3 args
  util/hist.h:748,757                     <- the #else stubs, 5 and 4 args

NO_ADDR has exactly one line in the tree: its own definition. Every
definition, every declaration and every call site is at the pre-ad83f3b7
arity. So the SLANG build is self-consistent and the no-SLANG build
cannot compile:

  builtin-annotate.c: In function 'hists__find_annotations':
  builtin-annotate.c:523:31: error: too few arguments to function
      'hist_entry__tui_annotate'; expected 4, have 3
    523 |     key = hist_entry__tui_annotate(he, evsel, NULL);
        |           ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  util/hist.h:757:19: note: declared here

Buildroot's package/linux-tools/linux-tool-perf.mk.in builds perf out
of the kernel tree it just built, and BR2_PACKAGE_SLANG is not set in
this image, so HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT is off and the stubs are what gets
compiled. BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_TOOLS_PERF is selected unconditionally by
package/mister-userspace/Config.in (T5 diagnostics), so this is not an
optional leg: it fails the whole `make all`.

Reverting restores the 6.18.51 arity, which is the arity the rest of
6.18.52 already uses. It is the smaller and safer of the two possible
repairs -- the other being to backport ad83f3b7 itself (55 insertions
across 5 files) plus the c2c feature commit its builtin-c2c.c hunks
build on, cd3466cd2639783da563253f1f9e3fb2ba936317 ("perf c2c: Add
annotation support to perf c2c report", 157 insertions), into a stable
tree that took neither and has no use for perf c2c annotation.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       e97bd4417010c648acf9b1e509cfb77fc506e09e, "perf annotate:
              Fix build with NO_SLANG=1", linux-6.18.y, authored by
              Namhyung Kim <namhyung@kernel.org>, committed by Sasha
              Levin <sashal@kernel.org> (AUTOSEL). It is a cherry-pick
              of mainline 0e6c07a3c30cdc4509fc5e7dc490d4cc6e5c241a.
              NOTE its `Fixes:` tag, which is a trap for anyone
              re-deriving this: it names cd3466cd2639783d ("perf c2c:
              Add annotation support to perf c2c report"), and THAT
              commit does not touch hist.h at all -- its two files are
              Documentation/perf-c2c.txt and builtin-c2c.c, and the one
              annotate call it adds uses the THREE-argument form. The
              commit that actually introduces `al_addr` and `NO_ADDR` is
              its immediate follow-up in the same series,
              ad83f3b7155db28e82de24dbaa1af2b8f5d972a3. What 6.18.y
              skipped is ad83f3b7; that is the SHA to check, not the one
              in the Fixes: line.
SKIPPED ONE:  6.18.y took mainline f06ba25ec54a3157c5b636483586f152f4478b9a
              ("perf annotate: Rename to __hist_entry__tui_annotate()"),
              the commit immediately BEFORE ad83f3b7, and it took
              0e6c07a3, the commit immediately after it (one unrelated
              commit, "perf report: Add comm_nodigit sort key", sits
              between them in mainline). It took the one before and the
              one after and skipped exactly the one in the middle --
              which is why 6.18.52 has the renamed symbol, has the
              repair, and has neither the parameter the repair repairs
              nor any user of NO_ADDR.
Author:       The reverted commit is Namhyung Kim's and is CORRECT where
              it was written. This revert is not a disagreement with it;
              it undoes a cherry-pick whose prerequisite is absent from
              the branch it was picked into. Authored here.
Upstream:     Mainline needs e97bd441/0e6c07a3 and must keep it --
              verified on v7.2.6's tools/perf/util/hist.h, where BOTH
              sides of the #ifdef agree at the al_addr arity (the
              HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT prototypes at :722 and :725 take
              `u64 al_addr`, and so do the #else stubs at :754 and
              :762). This revert must therefore NEVER be offered
              upstream and must never be applied to a tree that has
              ad83f3b7. It is scoped to 6.18.y, where the prerequisite
              is absent.
6.18-ONLY:    Deliberately absent from board/mister/de10nano/
              linux-patches-beta/series and from board/mister/de25nano/
              linux-patches/, both of which pin 7.2.x. MEASURED, not
              assumed: `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` against pristine
              v7.2.6's tools/perf/util/hist.h reports "Hunk #1 FAILED
              at 700. Hunk #2 succeeded at 741 (offset 2 lines). 1 out
              of 2 hunks FAILED", exit 1. Hunk 1's trailing context is
              the HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT prototypes, which on 7.2.6 already
              carry `u64 al_addr`. Hunk 2 DOES apply there (7.2.6's
              #else stubs are byte-identical to 6.18.52's), and that is
              precisely why listing this patch in a 7.x series would be
              a bug rather than a no-op: it would strip `al_addr` from
              stubs whose callers pass it. `patch` exits non-zero on
              the failed hunk 1, so Buildroot's apply-patches.sh stops
              the build at patch time -- same fail-closed shape as 0047
              and 0050, for a third distinct reason.
CONTEXT IS    The hunks are generated at -U12, not the usual -U3, and
DELIBERATE:   that is the opposite of what 449ce9a ("kernel: trim patch
              0032 context so it survives the 6.18.52 / 7.2.6 bumps")
              does, on purpose. 0032 is meant to SURVIVE bumps; this
              patch is meant to DIE at the right one. The extra context
              pulls the two HAVE_SLANG_SUPPORT prototypes into hunk 1,
              so the day 6.18.y takes cd3466cd2639783d (or reverts
              e97bd441 itself), this patch stops applying and CI fails
              in the linux-patch step naming this file, instead of
              applying cleanly and failing 20 minutes later as a perf
              compile error nobody associates with it. Buildroot patches
              at -F0, so there is no fuzz to absorb the mismatch.
RETIRES WHEN: 6.18.y fixes this on its own -- either by backporting
              ad83f3b7 or by reverting e97bd441 -- or when the pin
              leaves 6.18.y. All three show up the same way: this
              patch fails to apply. When that happens, DELETE IT; do not
              re-anchor it. Re-anchoring would re-break the build.
Forward-port: Verified at -F0 against 6.18.52's tools/perf/util/hist.h:
              applies standalone at zero offset, zero fuzz, and no other
              patch in this series touches tools/. The resulting file is
              byte-identical to 6.18.51's tools/perf/util/hist.h
              (`diff -u` between the patched 6.18.52 file and the
              pristine v6.18.51 one is empty), which is the arity CI
              built green on every run through 6.18.51.
Build-proof:  Not inferred from the arity -- compiled. The exact CI
              failure was first REPRODUCED on a host build of the
              pristine 6.18.52 tree with Buildroot's own flag set
              (NO_NEWT=1 NO_SLANG=1 NO_GTK2=1 NO_LIBPERL=1
              NO_LIBPYTHON=1 NO_LIBBIONIC=1 NO_SHELLCHECK=1 NO_LIBELF=1
              NO_DWARF=1 NO_LIBTRACEEVENT=1): same file, same line, same
              message ("builtin-annotate.c:523:31: error: too few
              arguments to function 'hist_entry__tui_annotate'; expected
              4, have 3"), make exit 2. With this patch applied and
              nothing else changed, the same command runs to
              "LINK perf", exit 0. A host x86-64 build is not the ARM
              cross-build CI does, but the failure is a C prototype
              mismatch in a header -- arch-independent by construction,
              and the arch-specific legs (arch/arm/, pmu-events) were
              never what broke.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
